in this lesson you're going to learn how  to find the reference angle when you're  given an angle in degrees or in radians  and we're going to talk about the  formulas that you can use to find that  reference angle so first let's do some  problems with degrees and then we'll do  some problems with radians so with  degrees what you want to do is when  you're working with an angle it's kind  of like a spinner right like if you spin  it this direction that's a positive  angle if you spin it clockwise that's  actually a negative angle but just say  for example here we're gonna spin it  counterclockwise if we spin it and you  end up in this first quadrant the  reference angle is just theta whatever  the angle is that they give you if you  end up over here in the second quadrant  you can take 180 minus the angle to get  your reference angle or if it's in  radians you do PI which is the same as  180 minus the angle if you're in the  third quadrant over here and you just do  the angle minus 180 or if you're in  radians you do the angle minus PI and  then if you end up in the fourth  quadrant you do 360 minus the angle or  two pi minus theta now if you don't want  to use the formulas what you can do is  wherever you end up like you know with  the spinner you spin it it can go around  as many times as you want on either  direction wherever you end up that's  called the terminal ray you always want  to drop an perpendicular to the x-axis  then you look at that angle that's  formed between the terminal ray and the  x-axis that's your reference angle it's  always between 0 and 90 if you're in  degrees or 0 and PI over 2 and you're if  you're in radians and it's always  positive so for example if I was ending  up here in the 3rd quadrant I drop my  perpendicular to the x-axis like that  and then that would be a reference angle  if you're over here you'd go up to the  x-axis and that would be a reference  angle so let's go through some examples  I think you'll get a better feel for  this say for example 120 120 we start  here it's positive so we're gonna spin  counterclockwise 90 a little bit more is  120 so what I could do is I could drop a  perpendicular and say how many more  degrees does it take me to get to that  x-axis that's going to be 60 degrees  notice this is in between 0 and 90 it's  positive that's our reference angle or  you could say well I'm in the second  quadrant let me just do 180  - the angle 180 minus 120 is 60 so you  can either use the formula or you can  just do a little bit more intuitively by  drawing a sketch that's what I like to  do for number two let's look at 600 600  is kind of interesting because 360 plus  another 180 that's how much 540 plus  another 60 is going to put you at 600  right so what we did is we went all the  way around and a little bit more right  but when we drop that perpendicular to  the x-axis that angle between the  terminal ray and the x-axis was a 60  degree angle now another way to do this  is you could say let's just subtract 360  from 600 so 600 minus 360 is 240 degrees  240 degrees is here in the third  quadrant right so if we take our angle -  180 240 - 180 is 60 degrees so you can  use the formula but these formulas only  really work when it's a positive angle  and it's in between 0 and 360 or 0 and  2pi so the third example I actually gave  us a negative angle negative a hundred  so that means we're actually gonna be  going what clockwise this time so we're  gonna go 90 + 10 more put you at  negative 100 if we drop a perpendicular  how many more degrees does it take to  get to the x-axis that's going to be 80  degrees not negative 80 because remember  the reference angle is always positive  always between 0 and 90 if you're  working in degrees but if you don't like  that method and you prefer the formulas  you could just say well I'm gonna add  360 adding 360 or subtracting 360 that's  just like going around you know one  direction or the other a full revolution  you're gonna end up in the same spot  that terminal ray so it's going to be  the same reference angle see here at 240  right and we're in the third quadrant so  again we could say theta minus 180 240  minus 180 is oh I made a mistake here  this is 260 degrees so we've got 260  minus 180 which is 80 degrees and you  can see that that's going to be your  reference angle so same idea now let's  do some in rate  okay number four we have 11 PI over six  sometimes what I like to do is when it's  improper like this how the numerator is  greater than the denominator is I like  to convert it into a mixed number so 6  goes into 11 once with 5 left over so  this is like one in 5/6 pi 1 pi we know  is 180 degrees right plus another 5 6 pi  right so almost 5 6 5 6 of pi is almost  on another 180 degrees so that puts us  right about here now if we drop it  perpendicular to that x-axis how much  more did it take to get to that x-axis  that's 1/6 pi all right so that's going  to be PI over 6 which is our reference  angle now if you want to use the formula  you can say well we're here in the  fourth quadrant we're in radians we  could take two pi minus theta so we can  say 2 pi minus our angle which is 11 PI  over 6 now 2 is like 2 over 1 if I  multiply the numerator and denominator  by 6 that's 12 PI over 6 minus 11 PI  over 6 which is 1 PI over 6 so you see  you're getting the same thing 1 is just  like more intuitive where you draw the  sketch let's look at number 5 now this  one's interesting because it's a  negative angle 8 over 3 that's improper  that's like negative 2 and 2/3 pi right  so negative angle we're going clockwise  negative 1 pi negative 2 pi and 2/3 of  Pi which you're right about there if we  drop a perpendicular to the x-axis it  takes another 1/3 pi right so our  reference angle is PI over 3 remember  it's always positive when you do the  reference angle even though this was a  negative angle the reference angle is  positive between 0 and PI over 2 PI over  2 is 90 but so between 0 and 90 degrees  there on PI over 2 in radians but if you  don't like that method you can see we  went around more than one revolution  right so what I could do is I could say  well negative 8 PI over 3 I want to make  this into a positive angle so let's just  say I add 2 pi ok 2 pi if I multiply the  numerator denominator by 3 that's 6 PI  over 3 plus negative 8 PI over 3 that's  a negative 2 PI over 3 it's still a  negative angle let me add  other revolution another 2pi so that's  really like adding six PI over three  plus negative 2 PI over 3 that's 4 PI  over 3 which is like 1 in 1/3 pi right  which makes sense see 1 and 1/3 pi but  if we're using our formulas now you can  see we're in the third quadrant and  we're in radians so theta minus PI  that's going to be our angle 4/3 PI or 4  PI over 3 minus PI which is like PI over  1 if we multiply the numerator and  denominator by 3 that's like 3 PI over 3  4 PI over 3 minus 3 PI over 3 is 1 PI  over 3 which is PI over 3 that's the  same thing we got here so you can use  the formula or draw a sketch number 6  the last one here 9 PI over 15  see 9 15 is what well 9 is a little bit  more than half of 15 right so that means  it's going to be a little bit more than  halfway to PI so it's going to be  somewhere right around here in the  second quadrant so we could say how much  much more does it take to get to 2 pi  here to the x-axis well that would be  another 6 PI over 15 which reduces to 2  PI over 5 so that would be our reference  angle just drawing a sketch but because  we're in the second quadrant we could  use the formula pi minus theta so pi  minus 9 PI over 15  pi is like PI over 1 if I multiply the  numerator denominator by 15 I get 15 PI  over 15 minus 9 PI over 15 which is 6 PI  over 15  which reduces to 2 PI over 5 and you  know you get the same result so either  draw a sketch or use the formulas and  you can find your reference angle now  what do we use reference angles for well  they're really handy when you're going  to find values of trig functions on the  unit circle and I talked about mastering  unit circle in that video right there so  follow me over that video and we'll get  some more practice with reference angles  as well as using the unit circle

